.TH TIMEOUT 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
timeout \- run a program with a time limit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timeout
.RB [ \-s
.IR signal ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR killsecs ]
.I secs
.I prog
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timeout
starts
.I prog
with the arguments
.IR arg ...
and waits for it.
If
.I prog
ends within
.I secs
seconds, so does
.BR timeout ,
with the exit status of
.IR prog .
Otherwise
.B timeout
sends
.I prog
a signal, by default
.BR TERM ,
and exits.
With
.BR \-k ,
it does not exit at once, but waits
.I killsecs
seconds more for
.I prog
to end, and sends
.B KILL
if it has not.
This
.B timeout
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
.B timeout
writes nothing itself on success, and
.I prog
shares its standard input, output, and error.
Errors are reported on standard error with a
.B timeout:
prefix.
.PP
.I prog
is started as the kit's shell starts an external command: an applet of
that name runs in a new busybox process, and any other name without a
directory part is looked up in
.BR PATH ;
see
.B Running commands
in
.BR busybox (1).
A file that begins with
.B #!\&
runs under the interpreter it names.
A shell function, or a shell built-in that is not also an applet, is not
found.
.PP
.I secs
and
.I killsecs
are durations: a decimal number of seconds, optionally followed by a
unit suffix that is
.B s
for seconds, the default,
.B m
for minutes,
.B h
for hours, or
.B d
for days.
A duration may have a fractional part, as in
.B 0.5
or
.BR 1.5h .
It may not have a sign, an exponent, or white space.
A duration without a period must not exceed 4294967295 once its suffix is
applied.
The wait is counted in whole milliseconds, and begins once
.I prog
has been created.
See
.BR sleep (1)
for the syntax and its error messages, but unlike
.BR sleep ,
.B timeout
does not accept
.BR inf .
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognized only before
.IR secs ,
and the first argument that is not an option is
.IR secs .
Everything after
.I prog
belongs to it, even arguments that begin with
.BR \- .
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-sKILL ,
or be the next argument.
An option given twice takes its last value.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BI "\-s " signal
Send
.I signal
instead of
.BR TERM .
A signal is a name, in any mix of uppercase and lowercase and with an
optional
.B SIG
prefix, or a number.
The signals are those of
.BR kill (1):
.B EXIT
(0),
.B HUP
(1),
.B INT
(2),
.B QUIT
(3),
.B ILL
(4),
.B FPE
(8),
.B KILL
(9),
.B SEGV
(11),
.B PIPE
(13),
.B TERM
(15),
and
.B ABRT
(22).
Any other name or number is rejected before
.I prog
starts.
.IP
Every signal except 0 ends
.IR prog ,
within the limits given in
.BR NOTES .
They differ in the exit code
.I prog
gets and in how
.B KILL
is delivered, as described in
.BR kill (1).
Signal 0 sends nothing, so
.I prog
keeps running.
.TP
.BI "\-k " killsecs
After sending the first signal, wait up to
.I killsecs
seconds for
.I prog
to end.
If it is still running, send
.B KILL
and exit with status 137.
If it ends in time, exit with its own exit status, not 124 or 137; see
.BR NOTES .
.I killsecs
is a duration, like
.IR secs .
The default, 0, waits for nothing and sends no second signal, as if
.B \-k
were not given.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
Later among the options it is an unknown option.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments of
.B timeout
are expanded when it starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
That includes the arguments of
.IR prog .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so a busybox
.I prog
does not expand them again.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Applets that are not run when named as
.IR prog ,
so that a program in
.B PATH
runs instead; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.I prog
is looked up when it is not an applet and has no directory part.
.PP
The startup code shared by all applets also reads
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
neither of which affects how
.B timeout
waits.
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.I n
.I prog
ended by itself within
.I secs
seconds, or within
.I killsecs
seconds after being sent a signal.
The status is the low 8 bits of its Windows exit code.
Since
.I prog
may exit with any value, the ones below do not prove that
.B timeout
produced them.
.TP
.B 124
.I secs
seconds passed and the signal was sent.
.B timeout
does not wait for
.I prog
to end, and does not know whether the signal worked.
This status is never used with a
.I killsecs
above 0.
.TP
.B 125
.B timeout
itself failed: an invalid option or option argument, an unknown signal,
an invalid duration, or a missing
.I secs
or
.IR prog .
.TP
.B 126
.I prog
was found, but Windows refused to run it because of an access error.
.TP
.B 127
.I prog
was not found, or could not be started for any other reason, such as a
file with a
.B .exe
suffix that is not a program.
A directory, or a file that is not executable, is not found.
.TP
.B 137
.B KILL
was sent last: either
.I secs
seconds passed with
.B \-s KILL
and without
.BR \-k ,
or
.I prog
was still running
.I killsecs
seconds after the first signal.
.PP
These errors are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
timeout: invalid number \(aq\fIargument\fR\(aq
timeout: number \fIargument\fR is not in 0..4294967295 range
timeout: unknown signal \(aq\fIname\fR\(aq
timeout: unknown option \-\- \fIc\fR
timeout: option requires an argument \-\- \fIc\fR
timeout: can\(aqt execute \(aq\fIprog\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
An option error is followed by the usage message.
A missing
.I secs
or
.IR prog ,
or a duration with a period that is not a number, such as a lone
.BR .\& ,
is reported with the usage message alone.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stop a build that takes more than ten minutes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ timeout 10m make
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a test for at most 5 seconds, and report when it times out:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ timeout 5 ./test.exe; test $? \-eq 124 && echo timed out
.EE
.RE
.PP
End a program after half a second, without letting it clean up:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ timeout \-s KILL 0.5 ./probe.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send
.B TERM
after a minute, and
.B KILL
5 seconds later if the program is still running:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ timeout \-k 5 1m ./server.exe
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR timeout :
.IP \(bu 2
The only options are
.B \-s
and
.BR \-k .
There are no long options, and no
.BR \-\-foreground ,
.BR \-\-preserve\-status ,
or
.BR \-v .
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I secs
of 0 does not disable the limit.
The signal is sent at once.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the signals above exist.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the process that
.B timeout
started is signaled, not its children or other descendants, and there
are no process groups.
A shell script that runs
.BR sleep (1)
as a separate process is ended, but the
.B sleep
keeps running.
.IP \(bu 2
Without
.BR \-k ,
.B timeout
exits as soon as it has sent the signal, and
.I prog
may still be running for a moment, or indefinitely.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-k ,
a
.I prog
that ends after the signal gives its own exit status, which is 0 when
the signal ended it, since its Windows exit code is the signal number
shifted left 24 bits, such as
.B 0x0F000000
for
.BR TERM .
GNU
.B timeout
reports 124.
.PP
The exit status holds 8 bits, so the exit code of
.I prog
is reduced modulo 256: 300 becomes 44, and 256 becomes 0, which looks
like success.
Nor does
.B timeout
translate a crash or a signal as
.BR sh (1)
does: a
.I prog
that crashes with the access violation 0xC0000005 gives 5, not 139, and
one ended by a signal gives 0, not 128 plus the signal number.
.PP
.B timeout
does not report a failure to send a signal.
A signal other than
.B KILL
is delivered asynchronously, by a thread injected into
.IR prog ,
which requires
.B timeout
and
.I prog
to be both 32-bit or both 64-bit.
When they are not, as with a 32-bit program started by the 64-bit
kit's
.BR timeout ,
every signal except
.B KILL
does nothing: after
.I secs
seconds
.B timeout
exits with status 124 and
.I prog
keeps running.
Use
.B \-s KILL
for such a program, or
.B \-k
to follow up with
.BR KILL .
.PP
The wait is passed to Windows in milliseconds, in 32 bits, and is not
checked, so it wraps around modulo 4294967296 milliseconds, which is 49
days, 17 hours, 2 minutes, and 47.296 seconds.
A
.I secs
of
.B 50d
times out after about 7 hours.
Keep durations under 49 days.
.PP
If
.B timeout
ends while
.I prog
is running, as when
.BR kill (1)
ends it with a signal other than
.BR KILL ,
it first sends
.B TERM
to
.IR prog .
Ending it with
.B KILL
gives it no chance to, and leaves
.I prog
running.
.PP
.I prog
may be given with forward slashes or backslashes, and with a drive
letter.
A
.I prog
with a directory part is not looked up in
.BR PATH ,
except in
.IR /bin ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
or
.IR /usr/sbin :
there an applet of the same base name runs even if the file exists,
and a missing file is looked up in
.B PATH
by its base name.
The suffix
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.B .cmd
may be left off.
.B timeout
does not ask Windows to elevate a
.I prog
that requires administrator rights, and fails to start it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR sleep (1)
